  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 117 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nAdditional showers/storms develop over the northern MX plateau \nand move east/northeast into the area Sunday morning through \nSunday afternoon as the mid to upper disturbance continues to dig \ninto the northern MX plateau into SW TX. Medium to high (45% to \n60%) rainfall chances will persist east of the Marfa Plateau and \nsoutheast of the SE NM plains, tapering off through the day as \ngreatest forcing shifts to the east with eastward development of \nthe disturbance away from the area. A 45% to 55% chance of \n0.50\"-0.75\" of rainfall through Monday morning is again present \nalong and south of I-20. While scattered heavy showers/storms will\nagain pose risk of ponding of water in low-lying and poor \ndrainage areas, Excessive Rainfall Outlook from WPC only indicates\na MRGL risk of heavy rainfall as on Saturday. Therefore, we are \nnot expecting major nor widespread flash flooding. Highs will only\nwarm a few degrees from Saturday as cloud ceilings slow lift and \nerode from northwest to southeast. Similarly, lows will not fall \nmuch lower than previous nights.\n\nEarly to the middle of next week in wake of the mid to upper \ndisturbance, ridging again builds over the Central CONUS, allowing\nfor a warming and drying trend. Highs rise back into the 60s F \nfor most of the area Monday, then into the 60s to mid 70s F \nTuesday, followed by solidly into the 70s F most locations apart \nfrom higher elevations by the middle of next week. Lows will \ncontinue to fall into the 40s and 50s F, so the diurnal spread of \n20F-25F will still be present, allowing for chilly but not \nfreezing nights each morning.",
